---
name: try-multistmt-body
description: a braceless `try` whose body has MORE THAN ONE statement — same-line catch, own-line catch, and a multi-statement finally
backends: [int, jvm, js]
---

# `try` with a MULTI-STATEMENT braceless body

`BUGS.md` `v2-front-try-in-def-body-shapes-break` shape (c). Shapes (a) and (b) were fixed and
verified; this one survived them because the differentiator is not where `catch` sits — it is
**how many statements the `try` body has**. A single-expression braceless body worked on every
lane; add one `val` and the whole program was miscompiled.

**Root cause, both self-hosted fronts:** `try` was not a **layout opener**. `try` ⏎
`<indented body>` therefore emitted neither a virtual `{` nor a `;` — the indented lines sit at
`v > ind` of the *enclosing* block, where the newline step returns the stack unchanged — so the
body's statements were glued onto the `try` line and `val` parsed as a *variable reference*
(`(let ((global val)) …)` on the legacy front, `(let ((lit (int 0))) …)` on F). One statement
survived only because gluing a single token changes nothing.

The case matters far beyond its own three functions: `std/agent.ssc`'s `postChatCompletionsOnce`
is exactly this shape, so a single parser gap silently removed `std/agent.ssc` — and everything
importing it — from the native lane.

`stillHere()` at the bottom is load-bearing rather than decorative. The failure mode of this bug
family is to leave the enclosing block open and **swallow the rest of the file**, so a regression
shows up as the program printing *less*, not as a mismatched value.

```scalascript
// (1) multi-statement body, `catch` on the same line as the arm
def divideMulti(a: Int, b: Int): String =
  try
    val q = a / b
    val label = "q=" + q
    label
  catch case e: Throwable => "caught-same-line"

// (2) multi-statement body, `catch` on its own line and the arm on the next
def divideMultiNl(a: Int, b: Int): String =
  try
    val q = a / b
    "q=" + q
  catch
    case e: Throwable => "caught-own-line"

// (3) all three clauses braceless, and a MULTI-statement finally. `finally` had to become a
// layout opener for the same reason as `try`; with `catch` braceless the arm parser also had to
// learn that `finally` ends an arm, or it swallowed the finally clause into the last arm body.
def withFinally(a: Int, b: Int): String =
  try
    val q = a / b
    "q=" + q
  catch
    case e: Throwable => "caught-finally-shape"
  finally
    println("cleanup-a")
    println("cleanup-b")

// (4) the braced form as a def body, for the sibling gap in the same family: `try` lexes as an
// identifier, so `try { … }` in STATEMENT position matched the `name { block }` call-with-thunk
// rule and lowered to `try(() => body)` — the legacy front's `unbound global: try`.
def divideBraced(a: Int, b: Int): String =
  try { (a / b).toString } catch { case _ => "caught-braced" }

def stillHere(): Int = 3

println(divideMulti(10, 2))
println(divideMulti(10, 0))
println(divideMultiNl(10, 5))
println(divideMultiNl(10, 0))
println(withFinally(9, 3))
println(withFinally(9, 0))
println(divideBraced(8, 4))
println(divideBraced(8, 0))
println(stillHere())
```
